.TH AXTUN 1 "15 September 2026" "axcall" "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
axtun \- IP over AX.25 on a TUN device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B axtun
.RI [ options ]
.I port
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B axtun
puts a network interface on the machine and carries what the kernel routes to
it over an AX.25 radio channel, so software that already exists works over
radio:
.BR ping ,
.BR ssh ,
UDP, and a route to a remote site's whole LAN.
.PP
This is a different thing from
.BR axsocks (1)
and
.BR axinetd (1),
which carry a stream between a socket and a connected-mode AX.25 session. Those
serve someone writing a packet application. This serves someone running IP
software that was never going to know what AX.25 is.
.PP
.B axtun
carries its own AX.25 layer and speaks KISS to a TNC over a serial port or over
TCP, exactly as
.BR axcall (1)
does. It needs no kernel AX.25 stack, no
.BR kissattach (8)
and no
.BR ax25rtd (8).
.PP
Be honest about the bitrate. A full frame at 1200 baud is about 1.7 seconds on
air, so at 1200 this is a curiosity; it becomes a tool at 9600 and above.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I port
The AX.25 port to use: a name from the ports file, a device path, or
.IR host : port
for a KISS-over-TCP modem. See
.BR axcall (1)
for the ports file.
.TP
.BI \-s " callsign" "\fR, \fP" \-\-mycall " callsign"
The callsign to transmit as. Required unless the ports file entry supplies one.
.TP
.BI \-\-dev " name"
The interface to create. The default is
.BR ax0 .
.TP
.BI \-\-addr " address" / length
Put this address on the interface, bring it up, and set its MTU, e.g.
.BR 44.131.20.1/24 .
Needs
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN .
Without it the interface is left exactly as it was found, which is the way to
run unprivileged; see PRIVILEGES.
.IP
The prefix length is not optional. It decides what is on-link, and guessing a
classful default would be wrong almost every time.
.TP
.BI \-\-mtu " bytes"
Interface MTU. The default is 236. Above 312 is allowed, with a warning: that
is the most the only peer yet tested will accept, not a limit of AX.25. See
SIZES. Only meaningful with
.BR \-\-addr ,
since without it the interface is not reconfigured at all.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read this config file instead of searching the usual paths.
.TP
.BI \-\-serial " device" [: baud ]
.TQ
.BI \-\-tcp " host" : port
Name the transport directly instead of giving a
.IR port .
.TP
.BI \-\-baud " rate"
Serial baud rate. Overrides a rate from the ports file. The default is 57600.
.TP
.BI \-\-txdelay " ms"
.TQ
.BI \-\-persist " 0-255"
.TQ
.BI \-\-slottime " ms"
.TQ
.BI \-\-txtail " ms"
KISS channel-access parameters, as
.BR kissparms (8)
set them per interface. Timers are in milliseconds and must be a multiple of
10, the KISS unit.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Log errors only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version, and the versions of the libraries built in.
.SH ADDRESSING
Use 44 net. AMPRNet is a live ecosystem with real allocations and real peers;
a private range makes this a demonstration rather than a connection to
anything.
.PP
Outbound addressing is resolved from the config file. A TUN device is
.BR NOARP :
the kernel hands a packet over and never asks who owns the address on this
link, so the route table is the whole of how a packet finds a station. That is
deliberate. Deciding at run time which transmitter to key, on the strength of
something a stranger said on the air, is not a decision to hand to a discovery
protocol.
.PP
Inbound is the opposite.
.B axtun
answers AX.25 ARP for its own address, because refusing to would leave it
undiscoverable by anyone who has not hand-configured it, which is most people.
A station heard on the air is remembered for an hour, and a station heard
through a digipeater is remembered with the reverse of the path it arrived by.
Anything in the config file beats anything heard.
.PP
When nothing knows how to reach an address,
.B axtun
broadcasts an ARP request for it, at most once every fifteen seconds per
address, and drops the packet that prompted it. Whatever sent it will retry,
and by then the answer will have arrived.
.SH ENCAPSULATION
IP goes out in UI frames with PID 0xCC, unacknowledged. The alternative,
running IP inside a connected-mode session, puts two retransmission timers on
the same loss: AX.25's T1 and TCP's RTO. TCP then measures a round trip that
includes the link layer's own retries, its estimator learns a number that has
nothing to do with the path, and throughput collapses.
.PP
On receive, both are accepted. A peer whose route says virtual-circuit mode is
a peer that exists, and refusing its frames would gain nothing.
.PP
ARP is PID 0xCD, in the AX.25 form: hardware type 3, seven-octet addresses,
thirty bytes. The protocol type field is sent as 0x00CC, which is AX25_P_IP
rather than the ETH_P_IP that RFC 826 would suggest: LinBPQ, XRouter and the
Linux kernel all send it, and the kernel discards an ARP request carrying
anything else without replying. It is accepted whatever it says on receive, and
a reply reflects what the request used.
.PP
Van Jacobson header compression (PID 0x06) is not implemented, and nothing
appears to need it. The specification defines the PID, but LinBPQ does not
implement it, XRouter's manuals never mention it, and JNOS, which is the stack
usually credited with it, wires its RFC 1144 codec to SLIP and PPP and not to
AX.25 at all.
.SH EGRESS FILTERING
.B axtun
decides what may be transmitted before transmitting it, and starts closed.
.PP
This is not a security feature and it is not a firewall. It is the thing that
stops a general purpose operating system using somebody's amateur licence to
announce its printers. Put an unfiltered IP interface on a shared radio channel
and the machine will transmit, unasked and forever: mDNS, LLMNR, SSDP,
NetBIOS, DHCP renewals, IPv6 router solicitations, NTP. On a wired LAN that is
background noise. Here it is a transmitter keying up on its own initiative
under someone's personal licence.
.PP
Rules live in the config file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
allow|deny <protocol> [to <range>] [port <n[\-m]>]
.fi
.RE
.PP
They are tried in order and the first match decides. Anything no rule matches
does not go out. The protocol is
.BR icmp ", " tcp ", " udp ,
a number, or
.BR any ;
the destination defaults to any; only tcp and udp rules may name a port.
.PP
Two things no ordinary rule can let through. A multicast, broadcast or
link-local destination needs a rule that names its range explicitly, because a
wildcard rule was written by somebody thinking about unicast. And anything that
is not IPv4 never reaches the filter at all, because a packet whose shape is
not understood cannot be judged; IPv6 on this interface is dropped.
.PP
A rule that names a port cannot match a trailing IP fragment, which does not
carry one. Such a fragment is dropped, so that a port rule cannot be evaded by
fragmenting around it.
.PP
With no rules at all a default policy applies: ping, TCP and UDP to a unicast
address, with the loud ports taken back out. That is not the strictest reading
of default-deny, but a tool whose first run looks broken teaches people to turn
the filter off.
.PP
The first time each kind of packet is dropped,
.B axtun
says so on standard error, once, naming what would have to be allowed.
.SH CONFIG FILE
.RS
.nf
# what reaches where
route 44.131.20.2      PN0TST
route 44.131.91.0/24   GB7RDG\-1 via WIDE1\-1
route default          GB7RDG\-1

# what may go out
allow icmp
allow tcp to 44.0.0.0/8
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read from
.I /etc/axcall/axtun
and then
.IR ~/.config/axcall/axtun ,
a route in the second replacing one for the same range in the first, or from
the single file named by
.BR \-\-config " or " $AXCALL_AXTUN .
.PP
Longest prefix wins, as in any routing table.
.B default
is the same as
.BR 0.0.0.0/0 .
.PP
.B via
gives a digipeater path, up to eight calls, comma separated. Each one costs
seven bytes of every frame and a whole extra transmission, so two is already a
long path for IP. Without it nothing behind a digipeater is reachable at all.
.SH PRIVILEGES
Creating a TUN interface needs
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN ,
and so does
.BR \-\-addr .
To run unprivileged, create the interface once as root and hand it over:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo ip tuntap add ax0 mode tun user $USER
sudo ip addr add 44.131.20.1/24 dev ax0
sudo ip link set ax0 up mtu 236
axtun \-s M0LTE\-7 radio
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B axtun
then attaches to the existing device, reads its address so it can answer ARP,
and changes nothing about it. A device created this way persists across
restarts of
.BR axtun ;
one created by
.B axtun
itself disappears when it exits.
.SH SIZES
Every number in this section comes from one implementation, LinBPQ 6.0.25.28.
None of it has been tested against JNOS, XRouter or the kernel's own AX.25
stack, so treat it as the shape of the default rather than as a property of
AX.25.
.PP
The largest frame LinBPQ will accept is 328 bytes. That is not from the
specification: it discards any KISS frame over 329 bytes including the type
byte, silently, with nothing on the air and nothing in its log. Each digipeater
in the path takes another seven bytes off what is left. Hence a default MTU of
236, which sits under all of it with room for a path; at 1200 baud the last
twenty bytes are not what is slowing anything down.
.PP
.B \-\-mtu
will go above that and print a warning rather than refusing, because a ceiling
measured against one peer should not be the reason a link that works cannot be
used. If the other end goes quiet after raising it, put it back first: a
station that discards an overlong frame without a word is indistinguishable
from one that has gone away.
.PP
LinBPQ splits its own output at an IP total length of 252 bytes, using ordinary
IP fragmentation rather than the AX.25 segmentation of PID 0x08, and pays no
attention to the PACLEN configured on the port.
.B axtun
hands fragments to the kernel and lets it reassemble them, which is what a TUN
device is for, so this needs nothing from either end.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped cleanly.
.TP
.B 1
Something unexpected went wrong.
.TP
.B 2
The command line or the config file was wrong.
.TP
.B 3
The interface or the modem could not be opened, or this is not Linux.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/axcall/axtun
System config file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/axcall/axtun
Per-user config file, read second.
.TP
.I /etc/axcall/ports
System ports file, shared with
.BR axcall (1).
.TP
.I /dev/net/tun
The control device interfaces are created through.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AXCALL_AXTUN
If set, the only config file consulted.
.TP
.B AXCALL_PORTS
If set, the only ports file consulted.
